#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# labeled

#% text
# `labeled(x, label)` is x with a label below it, as one graphic.

#% text
# `labeled(x, label, pos)` puts the label at `top`, `bottom`, `left` or `right`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# x may be a graphic, a plot or a string; the label may be any expression and takes `$...$` math. Around anything else (a function, data) `labeled` stays as it is: plots read it as the label of that curve or data set.

#% item
# `labeled([x1, x2, ...], [l1, l2, ...])` labels each part, in a row.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A plot with a caption:

#% cell
labeled(plot(sin(x), [x, 0, 6]), "$\\sin x$")
#% output
#= image("labeled.files/labeled-1.png")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Several parts:

#% cell
labeled([graphics(disk()), graphics(circle())], ["disk", "circle"])
#% output
#= image("labeled.files/labeled-2.png")

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# The label on the left:

#% cell
labeled(graphics(disk()), "a disk", left)
#% output
#= image("labeled.files/labeled-3.png")

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Around a function or data that is not a picture, `labeled` stays as it is, so plots read it: the label goes at the end of the curve:

#% cell
plot([labeled(sin(x), "sin"), labeled(cos(x), "cos")], [x, 0, 6])
#% output
#= image("labeled.files/labeled-4.png")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `framed`, `legended`, `grid`
